      ],
      "year": 2023,
      "abstract": "\nThis article aims to demonstrate that Fichte, in his work \u201eScience of Knowledge 1804-II,\u201c introduced a concept of transcendentality that leads to the proposition that the transcendental principle, which enables our understanding of objective reality, possesses a distinct sui generis mode of being. It is argued that this mode of being is expressed through Fichte\u2019s conception of \u2018pure knowledge\u2019. Through a closer examination of Fichte\u2019s idea of the need for a \u2018genetic\u2019 explanation of pure knowledge in the first four lectures of the Science of Knowledge 1804-II, it will be made explicit that the figure of transcendentality characterized as the \u2018being of knowledge\u2019 is not to be identified with an omnipresent subject-center. In conclusion, the conception of pure knowledge will be interpreted and reconstructed as a genetic field of the inner identity of knowledge: one that must be conceived not from a pure identity given a priori, but from the relationality between unity and duality \u2013 by virtue of this relationality, they always dynamically refer to each other and open up to each other despite their irreducible distance.",
